Subject: SUMMARY: Graphics Card Problem
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078608810358


 Many thanks to:

 Kurt Carlson, Dave Cherkus, Dave Golden, Patrick Farley,
 Amy Skowronek and Henry A. Flogel.

 Original Question:

|>   I recently installed DU 4.0b on a 2100 that was previously running
|>   Windows NT 3.51.  When the machine was running Windows the graphics
|>   were fine.  However, when I installed DU on the box it complained 
|>   with: "This system requires a driver for its graphics card."  At this
|>   point it the console will still work but in text mode.  

 
 Solution:

 All of the respondents had great advice but the problem was the graphics
 card is not supported by DEC Unix (or Open VMS for that matter). 
 Here's the card specs; 
 
 PB2GA-CA  NUMBER 9 VGA (1 - 3M) 

 DEC Support pointed me to a web page that had all this info:
 http://www.digital.com/info/alphaserver/tech_docs/alphasrv2100a/docs/supported_options.html

 Looks like we'll just trade up the card to finish the migration to DEC Unix.

Thanks,
Steve McLaughlin

From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Feb 18 17:47:16 1997
From: Pat Wilson <paw () northstar ! dartmouth ! edu>
Date: Tue, 18 Feb 1997 17:47:16 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: DECthreads problem
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078608810375


One of the developers here reports the following - is there a patch known?

Many thanks; I'll summarize.

Pat Wilson
paw@dartmouth.edu

------- Forwarded Message


I've run into a problem with DECthreads and disassociated programs:

There is a problem with the pthread libraries in DU 4.0B.  If an
exception isn't handled by code in the program, the DECthreads code
can end up writing the following sort of message out fd 1 or 2 (stdout
or stderr).  If the program has disassociated from its control
terminal, then fds 1 and 2 may be in use for other purposes (e.g. as
sockets), quite possibly in situations where the following message
causes some sort of problem for the program at the other end of the
socket.  I will probably add code to my program to open fds 1 and 2 to
/dev/null or some such, but I really don't think a system library
should write to stdout or stderr in any circumstances.  If it needs to
do something, perhaps syslog(3) would be a reasonable alternative.

DECthreads Last Chance handler: thread 1 exiting on status exception 0x177db005
Exception: Invalid memory address (dce / thd)

- -Eric Bivona
 Information Systems, Dartmouth College

